92186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186245151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246778 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "85984eba-7c96-50f1-b011-92e8b1639189" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247149 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247149 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247149 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247149 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/81/815f5ce1dd5bf3bc30b7853b53cd97736ffeb8224f30bc864cc7e43e07bedb5a.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247149 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3f37be5a-fb02-59a0-9bc3-1e609a53d6d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247149 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x-4y=1\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:x=-2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y,z⟩\\in {R}^{3}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49bd226a-4547-5889-a62c-e6c2912a1520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247159 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each subset.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. For instance, $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the equation $x-4y=1$ since $0-4\\cdot 0=0\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a plane, and a plane is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=-2y$ since $0=-2\\cdot 0.$ So $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace. No point in ${R}^{3}$ satisfies the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}<0.$ Hence, $C=∅.$ The empty set is not a subspace since it does not contain the zero vector.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315208 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315208 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315208 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ef4ac66-d953-5fba-ac6a-17597fb2834c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315349 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315349 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315349 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28317ab6-956f-51f4-9c07-7893dc4262d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315377 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315377 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315377 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c07460d-1eec-5672-90f4-