vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317349 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317349 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cc/ccb3b1dc1f175f6f018cb10e47db43a2ac5b4377c565dec4ee389b2a80455203.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "113a385d-0512-508f-a50c-e45bc26c3c1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317349 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317380 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317380 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317380 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317380 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317380 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92efc5e4-6fcf-5867-b774-9cceef4cda9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317380 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It actually represents the whole space ${R}^{2}$, since the coordinates of any vector $⟨x,y⟩$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=2y$ since $0=2\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321304 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321304 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321304 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "139a1cef-2533-5975-aee8-34b92cbec2da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321488 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321488 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321488 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c42cb6b-3a43-5e40-90aa-9faa026d0a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321679 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321679 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321679 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "437716e0-b97a-50de-ab6e-2583496fbbe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321743 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321743 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321743 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ba5e528-0a67-5866-8b57-6e4bd76ff209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322451 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322451 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322451 #edb/ref